Continuity Node Specification v0.1

Minimum viable node, custody packet, emergency authority, and red-team checklist

Status: Frozen test draft — revision requires recorded evidence from testing
Parent framework: The Mycelial Continuity Thesis

────────

Certification severity and decision framework

Severity levels

All findings, risks, control failures, and test results must be assigned one of the following severity levels:

• S0 — Informational: No material effect on safety, continuity, rights, custody, or certification. May be recorded for improvement and does not affect certification.
• S1 — Minor: Limited weakness with a credible workaround and no demonstrated material harm. Certification may proceed if the issue has an owner and due date.
• S2 — Major: Material weakness that could impair a critical function, record, safeguard, or governance control. Certification may proceed only with an accepted mitigation, compensating control, and defined retest.
• S3 — Critical: Demonstrated or credible risk of serious harm, coercion, irreversible loss, unsafe execution, concealed dependency, founder-only control, permanent emergency authority, or failure of a critical function without an effective fallback. Certification must be withheld, suspended, or revoked unless the validator formally determines that the finding has been contained and no prohibited condition remains.
• S4 — Disqualifying: A prohibited action, deliberate concealment, falsified evidence, retaliation against challenge, undocumented irreversible destruction, or intentional circumvention of certification controls. Certification must be denied or revoked. Reinstatement requires a new certification review and independent confirmation that the disqualifying condition has been remedied.

Severity must be based on credible impact, likelihood, reversibility, affected people or functions, and the availability of effective safeguards. A finding may be escalated when new evidence shows greater risk.

Treatment of unresolved risks

Every unresolved risk must be entered in the unresolved-risk register with:

☐ Description and affected function, person, record, or custody item.
☐ Severity level and rationale.
☐ Evidence and uncertainty.
☐ Immediate containment or protective action.
☐ Mitigation owner.
☐ Due date and retest date.
☐ Dependencies and required resources.
☐ Residual risk after mitigation.
☐ Acceptance authority.
☐ Review and expiry date.

Unresolved S0 risks may remain open without affecting certification. S1 risks may remain open only with an owner and due date. S2 risks require an accepted mitigation or compensating control before certification. S3 risks require containment and explicit validator approval; absent such approval, certification must be withheld or suspended. S4 risks cannot be accepted as ordinary residual risk and require corrective action before certification.

No person may accept a risk that primarily protects their own authority, access, custody, compensation, or reputation without independent review. Silence, inaction, or expiry of a due date does not constitute acceptance.

Mitigation acceptance

A mitigation is acceptable only when it:

☐ Addresses the stated cause or materially reduces the credible impact.
☐ Is specific, resourced, time-limited, and testable.
☐ Includes a compensating control where the underlying weakness remains.
☐ Preserves rights, safety, accessibility, records, and future options.
☐ Does not rely solely on the person, system, or authority that created the risk.
☐ Has been reviewed by an independent reviewer.
☐ Has evidence of implementation or a documented temporary control.
☐ Has a defined retest and expiry date.

A validator may accept a mitigation for S1 or S2 findings when the residual risk is documented and proportionate. Acceptance of an S3 finding requires a validator quorum and must state why certification remains safe, what controls prevent escalation, and when the decision expires. S4 findings may not be accepted through mitigation alone.

If a mitigation fails, expires, becomes impracticable, or creates a new material risk, the finding must be reopened and certification reassessed.

Certification authority

Certification decisions are made by an independent validator or authorised validator quorum. The quorum must include at least two qualified validators where capacity allows and must include an independent risk or governance reviewer for any S2 or higher finding. No validator may be the sole author, executor, custodian, function owner, or materially conflicted beneficiary of the matter being decided.

The node steward assembles the certification packet but cannot certify the node alone. Area owners attest to evidence but cannot approve their own unresolved S2 or higher risks without independent confirmation. The records steward preserves the decision, evidence index, dissent, and appeal record but does not determine the outcome.

Certification outcomes

A validator may issue one of the following outcomes:

• Certified: All applicable controls pass, no S3 or S4 condition remains, and unresolved risks are within accepted limits.
• Certified with conditions: The node may operate while specified S1 or S2 risks remain under accepted mitigation, compensating controls, owners, deadlines, and retest requirements.
• Provisional certification: Temporary certification granted after a major change, emergency, failed test, or incomplete evidence when immediate operation is necessary and protective controls are in place. Provisional certification must have a short expiry and cannot be renewed indefinitely without full review.
• Withheld: Certification is not granted because evidence is incomplete, a required control fails, or an unresolved risk exceeds the permitted threshold.
• Suspended: Existing certification is temporarily inactive because a critical condition, failed mitigation, overdue corrective action, material capability loss, or emergency-authority breach requires containment and review.
• Revoked: Certification is withdrawn because of a disqualifying condition, deliberate concealment, repeated failure to correct critical findings, or loss of the minimum safety and continuity basis.

Granting certification

Certification may be granted only after:

☐ The complete certification packet is assembled.
☐ Applicable checklist areas and evidence are reviewed.
☐ Findings are severity-rated.
☐ Unresolved risks and mitigations are recorded.
☐ Required tests are completed or formally deferred under an approved provisional decision.
☐ Conflicts and role overlaps are disclosed.
☐ The validator or quorum records the outcome, scope, conditions, expiry, and dissent.
☐ The records steward preserves the complete decision package.

The certification record must identify the certified node, covered functions and custody classes, exclusions, conditions, next review date, and authority of the decision.

Suspending certification

Certification must be suspended when:

☐ An S3 or S4 condition is discovered.
☐ A critical function loses its required fallback.
☐ A mitigation fails, expires, or is not retested by its deadline.
☐ Emergency authority exceeds its scope, duration, or review requirements.
☐ Evidence is materially falsified, concealed, or rendered unreliable.
☐ A custody item is executed, transferred, or destroyed outside authorised controls.
☐ The node can no longer protect life, agency, records, or essential continuity functions.

Suspension may be ordered by an authorised validator, emergency validator quorum, or explicitly designated protective authority when delay would create material harm. The order must state the trigger, scope, immediate controls, affected functions, review deadline, and restoration conditions. Suspension does not authorise destruction of records or abandonment of people or essential duties.

During suspension, the node may continue only those operations necessary for immediate safety, care, preservation of records, lawful obligations, and controlled remediation. A validator must review the suspension within the stated period and either lift it, continue it with conditions, or revoke certification.

Revoking certification

Revocation requires a recorded validator decision finding that certification cannot safely continue. The decision must identify the evidence, severity, failed safeguards, affected functions, and reasons lesser measures are inadequate. Revocation must preserve the node’s records, custody packets, challenge rights, and safe handoff or decoupling pathways.

A revoked node may apply for reinstatement only after:

☐ The cause of revocation is documented.
☐ Corrective actions are completed.
☐ Independent testing confirms effectiveness.
☐ A new certification packet is reviewed.
☐ Any prior dissent, appeal, or unresolved risk is addressed.
☐ A validator quorum grants new certification.

Appeal and review

The node may challenge a certification, suspension, or revocation decision through a recorded appeal. An appeal reviewer must be independent of the original decision and may not be the sole author, executor, or beneficiary of the disputed action. An appeal does not automatically restore certification, but temporary protective operation may be authorised where necessary to prevent disproportionate harm.

All certification decisions must be reviewed at least annually and immediately after a critical failure, major custody change, emergency-authority breach, material capability loss, node separation, failed mitigation, or credible challenge.

