2. Minimum viable node

A node passes only if every claimed critical function has a primary method, fallback, documented dependency, and recovery test.

2.1 Required capability checklist

☐ Food and water: Access, treatment, storage, allocation, and at least two sources or methods.
☐ Medical and care: First response, triage, medicine storage, care procedures, and one trained backup.
☐ Shelter and accessibility: Safe shelter, sanitation, mobility access, communication access, and alternative sleeping or care arrangements.
☐ Energy and communications: Low-power operation, charging, offline communication, and spare methods.
☐ Knowledge: Readable practical archive, local records, multiple media, and more than one custodian.
☐ Repair and tools: Maintenance, substitution, inventory, cross-training, and parts documentation.
☐ Governance and witness: Decision process, records, review, succession, and challenge pathway.
☐ Culture and education: Offline teaching, memory, recreation, and locally reproducible practices.
☐ Primary method identified.
☐ Backup or substitute identified.
☐ External dependency named.
☐ Failure response documented.
☐ Recovery test completed.

Pass: Every claimed critical function has a tested fallback or a named federated partner able to restore it.
Fail: Any critical function depends on one person, one device, one supplier, one location, or an undocumented assumption. A critical function without a credible fallback is S3 unless an accepted temporary protective arrangement is documented.

Evidence standard: Capability inventory, dependency map, dated procedures, named backups, and observed recovery tests with results.
Responsible roles: Function owner maintains evidence; backup practitioner verifies usability; independent reviewer examines the evidence; validator or designated review quorum confirms pass/fail findings and severity.
Review cadence: Quarterly for critical functions, after every failure or major change, and during the annual full-node review.
Required records: Capability register, procedures, inventories, dependency and federated-partner list, test plans, test results, corrective actions, unresolved-risk entries, and retest dates.

