3. Human capability and redundancy

For every continuity-critical function:

☐ Current practitioner identified.
☐ Secondary practitioner identified.
☐ Apprentice or learning pathway established.
☐ Procedure recorded in accessible form.
☐ Local substitute or federated partner identified.
☐ Recovery test completed.
☐ Role entry is voluntary and competence-based.
☐ Role exit, rotation, rest, and challenge are permitted.
☐ Accessibility barriers have been assessed and reduced.
☐ No role is treated as evidence of greater human worth.

Concurrent absence drill

☐ The node has simulated the absence of the originator, lead technical custodian, and records steward within one operating season.
☐ Critical functions were restored through backups, handoff packets, local substitutes, or federated partners.
☐ No new permanent authority was invented during the drill.
☐ Failures and recovery times were recorded.

Pass: Defined functions and records remain recoverable without founder-only access.
Fail: Any critical function or authority collapses because one person is unavailable. This is at least S3 when the function is critical.

Evidence standard: Current role matrix, accessible procedures, training records, voluntary participation confirmations, and witnessed absence-drill results.
Responsible roles: Function owner and apprentice maintain function evidence; accessibility reviewer checks barriers; independent reviewer assesses dependency and authority risks; records steward preserves the drill record; validator assigns severity where failure affects certification.
Review cadence: Role coverage monthly, training quarterly, and concurrent absence drill at least annually or after major turnover.
Required records: Role matrix, competency and training log, handoff packet, accessibility assessment, drill scenario, attendance, recovery times, failures, corrective actions, severity ratings, and retest dates.

