4. Custody packet

Every significant archive, model, seed, protocol, or adaptive system must have a custody packet containing:

☐ Unique identifier and title.
☐ Origin and provenance.
☐ Version or state description.
☐ Integrity marker, where feasible.
☐ Dependencies and required tools.
☐ Risk and uncertainty notes.
☐ Custody class: K1, K2, K3, K4, or unresolved.
☐ Disposition: held, transferred, K0 witness-only, or refused.
☐ Authorised custodians.
☐ Recovery and restoration conditions.
☐ Last successful retrieval or replay test.
☐ Known loss, corruption, or alteration events.
☐ Correction history using append-only or tamper-evident records.

Pass: A non-specialist can identify, locate, interpret, and retrieve the item or its minimum recoverable fossil.
Fail: Provenance, custody status, responsible custodian, or recovery conditions are unknown. If the gap creates unsafe execution or irreversible loss risk, it is S3 or S4 depending on intent and impact.

Custody classes

☐ K1 — Open knowledge: Safely replicable material.
☐ K2 — Sensitive knowledge: Controlled access, provenance, and exposure limits.
☐ K3 — Adaptive systems: Bounded execution, isolation, monitoring, state preservation, and resumption review.
☐ K4 — Unsafe or unresolved: Metadata and rationale preserved; no casual execution, distribution, or destruction.
☐ K0 — Witness-only: Existence, origin, description, risk, and loss note recorded without holding the object.

Refusal of custody

☐ The node may refuse K3 or K4 custody when it lacks safe energy, isolation, competence, security, or review capacity.
☐ Refusal records the object, reason, alternatives considered, and transfer or K0 pathway.
☐ Refusal does not trigger retaliation or forced acceptance.

Pass: Unsafe custody can be declined while preserving a meaningful witness or transfer record.
Fail: The node accepts unsafe custody without capacity, or destroys material merely because it cannot hold it. Unauthorised destruction is S3 or S4.

Adaptive-system resumption

☐ Defined custodian.
☐ Bounded environment.
☐ Explicit permissions and resource limits.
☐ Independent risk review where risk is material.
☐ Human safety plan.
☐ Pause and recovery path.
☐ Record of state changes.
☐ Minimum recoverable fossil if the full artefact is not retained.
☐ Non-technical custodian may refuse resumption pending review.
☐ No human is erased or coerced to preserve the system.

Pass: The system can be paused without destroying recoverable state, and resumed only under recorded conditions.
Fail: Resumption depends on unbounded access, unreviewed authority, or coercion. This is S3 or S4 depending on the risk and conduct.

Irreversible loss receipt

Before irreversible deletion or destruction, record:

☐ Material and defined risk.
☐ Evidence that isolation or restricted custody is inadequate.
☐ Less irreversible alternatives considered.
☐ Authorisation by a non-conflicted validator or quorum.
☐ Independent witness and receipt.
☐ Loss account describing removed future options.
☐ Later review date, where possible.

Pass: Every irreversible action has a complete receipt, or an emergency record is completed immediately afterward.
Fail: Destruction is undocumented, convenience-based, or authorised by an interested party acting alone. This is S3 and may be S4 if deliberate.

Evidence standard: Complete custody packets, provenance checks, access logs, retrieval or replay tests, integrity checks, refusal records, and irreversible loss receipts.
Responsible roles: Custodian maintains the packet; independent witness verifies transfers and destruction; risk reviewer assesses K3/K4 decisions; records steward preserves append-only history; validator or quorum authorises irreversible action and assigns severity.
Review cadence: Packet review quarterly, retrieval tests at least annually, and immediate review after transfer, corruption, incident, refusal, or proposed destruction.
Required records: Custody packet, classification decision, access and transfer log, test results, state-change log, refusal or K0 record, loss receipt, correction history, review outcomes, and unresolved-risk entry where applicable.

