5. Emergency authority

Every emergency measure must specify:

☐ Trigger.
☐ Defined harm.
☐ Smallest permitted action.
☐ Authoriser.
☐ Executor.
☐ Maximum duration.
☐ Expiry condition.
☐ Review point.
☐ Restoration pathway.
☐ Record and notification requirements.

Emergency authority must not:

☐ Amend the fundamental charter.
☐ Destroy or conceal its own records.
☐ Create permanent classes.
☐ Treat dissent as proof of harm.
☐ Rewrite custody classes for convenience.
☐ Promote K4 material to K3 without review.
☐ Conscribe labour beyond the stated task and duration.
☐ Become permanent through silence.

If the validator quorum is unavailable:

☐ Only the minimum temporary protective action is taken.
☐ Automatic expiry is set.
☐ Later validator or independent review is mandatory.
☐ The action is recorded with uncertainty and alternatives.

Pass: Every emergency power is narrow, time-limited, reviewable, and transferable.
Fail: Any emergency measure has no expiry, no review, no record, or no restoration path. Unauthorised permanence or concealment is S3 or S4.

Evidence standard: Contemporaneous authority record showing trigger, harm, scope, duration, authorisation, execution, notification, and outcome.
Responsible roles: Authoriser defines scope; executor records implementation; records steward preserves the log; independent reviewer or validator quorum conducts retrospective review and assigns severity.
Review cadence: At activation, at each stated review point, immediately upon expiry, and within a defined short period after any quorum-unavailable action.
Required records: Emergency order, trigger evidence, action log, notifications, expiry confirmation, restoration record, retrospective review, dissent or challenge record, mitigation plan, and severity decision.

