7. Capacity-constrained triage

☐ Criteria are defined before crisis where feasible.
☐ Immediacy of life-threatening need is considered.
☐ Stabilisation likelihood is considered.
☐ Dependants and vulnerable people are protected.
☐ Essential services are considered without granting personal privilege.
☐ Alternatives are assessed.
☐ Comparable cases use a queue, lottery, or other consistent method.
☐ Political loyalty, wealth, ethnicity, disability, fame, and ideological conformity are excluded.
☐ Every priority decision has a review point.
☐ Temporary priority cannot become permanent rank.

Pass: Allocation decisions are specific, documented, reviewable, and tied to need or defined continuity function.
Fail: Role, status, identity, or loyalty becomes a hidden permanent claim on essential resources. Deliberate discriminatory or coercive allocation is S3 or S4.

Evidence standard: Predefined criteria, case records limited to necessary information, alternatives considered, allocation rationale, and comparable-case checks.
Responsible roles: Triage lead applies criteria; care or safeguarding reviewer checks equity; records steward preserves decisions; affected people may request review; validator or designated reviewer resolves escalated disputes and assigns severity.
Review cadence: Criteria before crisis, decisions at each allocation cycle, and retrospective review after the crisis or any disputed decision.
Required records: Criteria register, queue or lottery record, allocation decision, alternatives assessment, conflict declaration, review request, outcome, expiry of temporary priority, and any mitigation or severity record.

