8. Federation and handoff

☐ Node identity and protocol version are recorded.
☐ Capability inventory is exchangeable.
☐ Resource offers and requests are recorded.
☐ Archive manifests are exchangeable.
☐ Active dependencies are visible.
☐ Safety and integrity alerts are supported.
☐ Custody and handoff records are supported.
☐ Decouple notices are supported.
☐ Dispute records are preserved.
☐ Succession and contact updates are supported.
☐ Records are readable offline.
☐ A node can export its records and leave or fork.
☐ Shared obligations can continue during partial separation.
☐ Reconnection does not require erasing history.

Pass: Nodes can cooperate, pause joint operations, separate, and reconnect without coercion or loss of their own records.
Fail: Federation requires one central owner, proprietary platform, permanent network, or surrender of local governance. A failure that prevents safe exit or causes material record loss is S3.

Evidence standard: Successful export/import tests, offline readability checks, signed handoff and decouple records, dependency disclosures, and reconciliation tests that preserve history.
Responsible roles: Federation liaison coordinates exchange; local records steward verifies exports; receiving custodian confirms handoff; dispute reviewer handles contested records; validator or quorum confirms disputed governance outcomes where required and assigns severity.
Review cadence: Protocol and contact review quarterly, handoff tests at least annually, and immediate review after separation, reconnection, or integrity alert.
Required records: Node identity record, protocol version, capability and archive manifests, offers and requests, dependency map, handoff receipt, decouple notice, dispute log, export package, reconnection record, and unresolved-risk entry where applicable.

