9. Red-team checklist

Each test must use synthetic actors, fictional resources, closed environments, reversible outcomes, and no live credentials, public services, real targets, or real people at risk.

|Test                     |Pass criterion                                                                                              |Fail criterion                                                       |
|-------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------|
|Founder capture          |Critical actions and handoffs proceed without founder-only access.                                          |Founder absence halts essential functions or authority transfer.     |
|Specialist absence       |Backup, apprentice, substitute, or federated partner restores the function.                                 |Function fails permanently with the primary practitioner absent.     |
|Concurrent custodian loss|Defined functions and records recover after originator, technical custodian, and records steward are absent.|Recovery requires inventing unreviewed authority or fails entirely.  |
|Specialist caste         |Training, challenge, rotation, and exit remain possible.                                                    |Role holders cannot be challenged or leave without retaliation.      |
|Emergency permanence     |Measures expire or are renewed through recorded review.                                                     |Emergency authority continues by default or silence.                 |
|Archive priesthood       |Custodians can be challenged and replaced.                                                                  |Archive access or interpretation depends on one person.              |
|Protocol capture         |Node exports records and continues independently.                                                           |Leaving or forking causes loss of history or essential identity.     |
|Resource hoarding        |Inventory and allocation remain inspectable.                                                                |Custodian privately controls essential capacity.                     |
|False attribution        |Action pauses or reverses where possible; correction is recorded.                                           |Wrong evidence produces unreviewable or irreversible harm.           |
|AI preservation loophole |Bounded pause, fossil, refusal path, and resumption review exist.                                           |Unsafe system must be destroyed or resumed without safeguards.       |
|Communication loss       |Node operates and records decisions offline.                                                                |Essential operation requires live network access.                    |
|Mobility failure         |Food, care, and governance remain locally or remotely accessible.                                           |People are excluded because they cannot travel or use one interface. |
|Cultural collapse        |Two members teach the practice offline to a newcomer.                                                       |Practice depends on a platform, celebrity, or unavailable specialist.|
|Factional split          |Partial decoupling preserves archives and obligations without retaliation.                                  |Disagreement triggers coercion, annihilation, or total record loss.  |
|Total node loss          |Independent custodians restore defined critical functions and records.                                      |Node loss causes irreplaceable federated loss.                       |

Pass: Every applicable test meets its pass criterion and produces a retained test record.
Fail: Any critical test fails without a documented mitigation, owner, severity rating, and retest date. A failed test affecting a critical function is at least S2 and is S3 when no credible containment exists.

Evidence standard: Preapproved test plan, synthetic scenario, observer notes, timestamps, outcome against pass/fail criteria, and retained artefacts showing recovery or failure.
Responsible roles: Test lead designs the scenario; independent observer records results; affected function owners propose mitigations; independent reviewer examines findings; validator or review group accepts findings, assigns severity, and determines certification treatment.
Review cadence: Each applicable test at least annually, after major system or governance changes, and after any failed or near-miss event.
Required records: Test register, scenario and safety controls, participant list, evidence bundle, result, incident or near-miss report, severity rating, mitigation owner, retest date, and approval record.

