11. Compact pass/fail charter

A node passes certification only if:

☐ It preserves life, knowledge, care, capability, culture, and future choice.
☐ It declares dependencies and maintains tested fallbacks.
☐ It plans around functions rather than indispensable people.
☐ It maintains custody packets for significant preserved material.
☐ It can refuse unsafe custody without destroying the witness record.
☐ It uses bounded, witnessed, reviewable emergency authority.
☐ It separates decision functions where feasible.
☐ It protects autonomy, dignity, accessibility, and voluntary participation.
☐ It prevents temporary triage from becoming permanent class.
☐ It can operate offline.
☐ It can hand off roles, records, and custody.
☐ It can federate, decouple, fork, and reconnect.
☐ It has completed the red-team tests or documented mitigations and retest dates.
☐ All findings are severity-rated and entered in the appropriate register.
☐ No unresolved S3 or S4 condition remains unless explicitly contained and accepted under the certification framework.
☐ Any conditional or provisional certification has an owner, expiry, compensating control, and retest date.

Fail certification if: Any node relies on coercion, permanent emergency power, founder-only authority, hidden custody, irreversible undocumented action, private control of essential resources, or a single irreplaceable person, system, archive, or location. Certification must also be withheld or suspended for any S3 condition lacking accepted containment, any S4 condition, falsified or materially unreliable evidence, or failure to implement an accepted mitigation by its deadline.

Evidence standard: Certification evidence must cover every applicable checklist area, include current records and test results, identify and severity-rate unresolved risks, document mitigation acceptance or rejection, and be independently reviewable.
Responsible roles: Node steward assembles the certification packet; area owners attest to their evidence; independent validator or quorum makes the certification, suspension, or revocation decision; records steward preserves the decision, conditions, dissent, appeal, and complete evidence index.
Review cadence: Initial certification, annual recertification, and immediate reassessment after a critical failure, major custody change, emergency-authority breach, node separation, material capability loss, failed mitigation, or credible challenge.
Required records: Certification checklist, evidence index, area attestations, test and mitigation register, unresolved-risk register, severity decisions, validator decision, dissent or appeal record, certification status, scope, conditions, certification date, expiry or next review date, suspension or revocation record where applicable, and corrective-action plan.

